it's a Bittersweet day for Gloria three  years after her father George  chandrasekharan was killed with 268  others in churches and hotels across Sri  Lanka's capital on Easter Sunday there's  some accountability as a citizen of this  country I'm happy because at some point  we got a Justice it's kind of a winning  Wicket for us but as a daughter of  George I'm not happy because I may get  get million billion or trillions but  those that money cannot bring my Dada  back on Thursday the Supreme Court ruled  former Sri Lankan president maitripala  sirisena and four senior security  officials violated the rights of the  bomb victims by failing to protect them  they were ordered to pay hundreds of  thousands of dollars in compensation to  the victims families the then Secretary  of the ministry of Defense the police  chief and heads of the two main  intelligence agencies were the others  who were fined at the center of the  petitions filed in court was the fact  that the government had received  intelligence regarding a possible attack  nearly three weeks before the bombings  and did nothing to prevent them the  court said we conclude that the former  president sirisena has been lacks in  affording the protection and guarantees  in joined under the Constitution and  other laws and he has breached his duty  to protect adding that this dismal  failure on the part of the former  president sirisena resulted in  disastrous consequences for this country  ahead of the attacks the government was  divided after president sirisena's  failed attempt to sack his prime  minister the now President Ronald Vikram  singer and replace him with the defeated  president behind the rajapak sir many  here believe this division may have  affected crucial aspects like National  Security  observers say the two camps were so busy  fighting each other they failed to act  on valuable intelligence the Supreme  Court acknowledged this fact saying  there a specificity exactitude and  clarity as to the likely attackers modes  of attack and their targets the victims  families welcomed the decision but say  there's more work to be done to find  those who carried out those attacks and  bring them to justice a few days after  the bombings isil claimed responsibility  but many doubt that claim the government  has filed more than 23 000 charges  against a group of at least 40 suspects  accusations of possible involvement by  intelligence operatives claims of  cover-ups and allegations by the  Archbishop of Colombo that the bombings  were part of the plot to bring the  rajbak's brothers back to Power have not  been investigated I do want Justice  please I can sleep peacefully  we as victims Cry For Justice like  Gloria many victims say they will  continue their campaign until they find  the truth Minna Fernandez Al Jazeera  Colombo
 

